<!-- Icon-pack component map, read by Trebuchet's
     IconPackProvider.parseAppFilter (packages/apps/Trebuchet/src/com/android/
     launcher3/lineage/icon/providers/IconPackProvider.java:159-171), which
     resolves it with getIdentifier("appfilter","xml",pkg). res/xml ONLY;
     assets/appfilter.xml is ignored by this launcher.

     MATCHING IS EXACT ComponentName, not package. IconPack.getIcon looks up
     name.flattenToString() only (IconPack.java:105-107), so the package-only
     key IconPackProvider also stores is dead. An app that renames its launcher
     activity needs a second row here.

     An app with no row keeps its OWN icon, unmodified: IconPack.getDrawable
     returns null (:113-119) and CustomIconProvider falls back (:38-47). That is
     the right outcome for a modern app, which already ships a proper adaptive
     icon; overriding it with a flat bitmap from a pack would make it worse,
     not better. This file therefore themes exactly two things: the applications
     THIS ROM ships, whose stock icons are the ones worth replacing, and the
     applications the supplied pack itself chose to map.

     NOTHING HERE IS INVENTED. Every component in the first block was read off
     the running handset with
       cmd package query-activities -a android.intent.action.MAIN \
                                    -c android.intent.category.LAUNCHER
     except com.android.terminal, which is verified from its own manifest in
     this tree. An earlier revision of this file carried 33 hand-written rows
     for global applications such as Telegram, Spotify and Gmail; those
     component names were guesses, and those applications ship perfectly good
     adaptive icons of their own. They are gone. -->
